From: bornaradusin <bornaradusin@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 10:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] wsi/videoout: An older window takes the display back from one
 left behind

A window's picture smaller than the owner's was always refused, so a
movie's window inside a game's doesn't take the display (Blade & Sword).
Ruined King, changing its resolution, shows a 3840x2160 picture once on a
window of its own and then leaves that window; its own window's next
picture, 2560x1080 or 1920x1080, was refused for being smaller, and the
game stayed on its last picture for good.

Once the owner has stopped presenting for half a second, a picture on an
older window now takes the display whatever its size: that window is the
game's own, going on after the newer one was left. A newer smaller
window, a movie's, is refused as before, and helpers' strips are too.
---
 src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_common_videoout_takeover.h | 12 ++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_common_videoout_takeover.h b/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_common_videoout_takeover.h
index 5a406ac..48e14ee 100644
--- a/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_common_videoout_takeover.h
+++ b/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_common_videoout_takeover.h
@@ -24,6 +24,13 @@
  * either rule: a DirectShow movie's video window, a framed window inside a
  * fullscreen game's (Blade & Sword: its 634x448 movie window took the display
  * from the game's 640x480 one, and the game stopped with no picture).
+ *
+ * Except an older window's picture, of any window's size, once the owner on a
+ * newer window stopped presenting: that window was left behind and the game
+ * goes on in its own. Ruined King, changing its resolution, shows a 3840x2160
+ * picture once on a window of its own and leaves it; its own window's next
+ * picture, 2560x1080 or 1920x1080, was refused for being smaller, and the game
+ * stayed on its last picture for good.
  */
 
 #ifndef WSI_COMMON_VIDEOOUT_TAKEOVER_H
@@ -47,7 +54,7 @@ enum wsi_videoout_takeover {
    WSI_VIDEOOUT_REFUSED,  /* the owner keeps the display */
    WSI_VIDEOOUT_FREE,     /* nothing has it, the new one replaces the owner, or the owner never presented */
    WSI_VIDEOOUT_NEWER,    /* a newer window's picture covering the owner's */
-   WSI_VIDEOOUT_STALE,    /* a picture covering the owner's, which stopped presenting */
+   WSI_VIDEOOUT_STALE,    /* a picture covering the owner's, or an older window's, the owner stopped presenting */
 };
 
 /* A window's picture, not a helper window's (wined3d's 10x10 probe, a 113x2 strip). */
@@ -67,7 +74,8 @@ wsi_videoout_takeover(const struct wsi_videoout_window *owner, bool replaces, co
    if (!wsi_videoout_window_sized(chain->width, chain->height))
       return WSI_VIDEOOUT_REFUSED;
    if (chain->width < owner->width || chain->height < owner->height)
-      return WSI_VIDEOOUT_REFUSED;
+      return chain->age < owner->age && now_ns - owner->present_ns >= WSI_VIDEOOUT_STALE_NS ? WSI_VIDEOOUT_STALE
+                                                                                              : WSI_VIDEOOUT_REFUSED;
    if (chain->age > owner->age)
       return WSI_VIDEOOUT_NEWER;
    if (now_ns - owner->present_ns >= WSI_VIDEOOUT_STALE_NS)
